class:: MarkovChain
summary:: Triggered finite-state Markov chain from a live transition Buffer
categories:: UGens>Control>Markov
related:: Guides/Stochastica, Classes/SemiMarkov, Classes/MultiGaussNoise

description::

A Markov Chain is a fundamental stochastic model for state transitions. Mathematically, it moves through a finite set of discrete states where the probability of moving to any next state depends solely on the current state, dictated by a transition matrix. It has no memory of the distant past. Conceptually, this is the ultimate structural tool for generative sequencing and algorithmic composition, allowing you to explicitly design the harmonic or rhythmic tendencies of a sequence (e.g., specifying that a C major chord has a 70% chance to resolve to G).

Samples the current row of a row-major transition-weight matrix on each rising trigger. Negative or non-finite weights become zero for sampling, rows need not sum to one, and a zero row holds state. The user's Buffer is never rewritten.

This implements a standard finite-state Markov chain. Like link::Classes/Stepper::, it exposes and holds a discrete state index; unlike Stepper, the next state is sampled from live matrix weights.

subsection:: Transition and state view

image::markov-chain-dynamics.png#Left: a three-state row-major transition matrix, where darker cells have greater weight. Right: one sampled state sequence; horizontal runs occur because the diagonal self-transition weights are strongest.::

classmethods::

method:: ar
argument:: numStates
Init-rate state count, clipped to 1 through 256.
argument:: trig
Sample one transition on a rising edge.
argument:: transitionBuf
One-channel Buffer containing code::numStates*numStates:: row-major weights.
argument:: valueBuf
Optional one-channel Buffer with one value per state; negative uses the state index.
argument:: initialState
Rounded and clipped initial zero-based state.
argument:: seed
Init-rate local seed.
argument:: reset
Restore initial state and seed on a rising edge.
returns:: State index and mapped value.

method:: kr
Control-rate version with the same arguments and outputs.

instancemethods::

examples::

code::
(
Routine {
    b = Buffer.loadCollection(s, [0.9, 0.1, 0.2, 0.8]);
    s.sync;
    x = { MarkovChain.kr(2, Impulse.kr(4), b)[0].poll }.play;
}.play;
)
::

code::
(
Routine {
    b = Buffer.loadCollection(s, [
        0.6, 0.3, 0.1,
        0.2, 0.5, 0.3,
        0.1, 0.3, 0.6
    ]);
    c = Buffer.loadCollection(s, [220, 330, 550]);
    s.sync;
    x = {
        var freq = MarkovChain.kr(3, Impulse.kr(6), b, c)[1];
        SinOsc.ar(freq, 0, 0.08)
    }.play;
}.play;
)
::

code::
// Generative Algorithmic Chord Changes
(
Routine {
    var matrix;
    matrix = Buffer.loadCollection(s, [
        0.1, 0.7, 0.1, 0.1,
        0.1, 0.1, 0.7, 0.1,
        0.1, 0.1, 0.1, 0.7,
        0.7, 0.1, 0.1, 0.1
    ]);
    s.sync;
    {
        var trig, state, freqs, sig;
        trig = Impulse.kr(2);
        // Correct signature: numStates, trig, transitionBuf
        state = MarkovChain.kr(4, trig, matrix)[0];
        freqs = Select.kr(state, [
            [60, 64, 67], // C Major
            [65, 69, 72], // F Major
            [67, 71, 74], // G Major
            [57, 60, 64]  // A Minor
        ]).midicps;
        sig = Splay.ar(Saw.ar(freqs)) * EnvGen.kr(Env.perc(0.1, 0.4), trig);
        sig * 0.2
    }.play;
}.play;
)
::
